+</defs></svg><main class="poster"><header><h1>Building Legal RAG: what each component contributes</h1><img class="logo" src="assets/trier-university.svg" alt="Universität Trier"></header>
+<div class="columns">
+<div class="col left">
+<section id="s1" class=""><h2><span>1.</span> Research Context</h2>
+<div class="content"><p class="">RAG grounds language-model answers in external documents, but legal QA requires a pipeline to satisfy two goals at once: retrieve enough relevant evidence to produce a correct answer, while keeping source-page citations precise and complete. This is challenging because legal PDFs may lack machine-readable text, evidence can appear at different textual scales, and questions may depend on either exact legal references or semantic similarity. Moreover, retrieving more evidence can improve coverage while also introducing irrelevant source pages. We therefore examine how design choices in document processing, retrieval, and attribution can improve answer quality without sacrificing citation quality.</p>
+</div></section>
 
-<section aria-labelledby="hypothesis-title">
- <div class="section-title"><span class="num">01</span><h2 id="hypothesis-title">Context and hypothesis</h2></div>
- <p class="context">Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) grounds language-model answers in external documents [1]. Legal QA needs correct answers and precise, complete source-page citations [2, 3]. PDFs may lack searchable text; evidence spans different text scales and may require exact legal terms. Retrieving more passages can also introduce irrelevant pages.</p>
- <p class="question">How can a legal RAG pipeline improve answer quality<br>while keeping source-page citations both precise and complete?</p>
- <div class="grid three hypotheses">
-  <div><h3>H1 · Retrieval</h3><p>Multiple text scales, hybrid search and reranking will improve answers and evidence retrieval over R0.</p></div>
-  <div><h3>H2 · Scan robustness</h3><p>Recovering text from scanned documents will reduce the performance loss caused by the missing text layer.</p></div>
-  <div><h3>H3 · Citation selection</h3><p>Selecting pages after answer generation will improve citation precision while retaining most of the relevant evidence.</p></div>
- </div>
-</section>
+<section id="s2" class=""><h2><span>2.</span> Prior Work &amp; Study Focus</h2>
+<div class="content"><p class="">Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) conditions language-model answers on retrieved documents [1]. Legal retrieval must balance precision and coverage: LegalBench-RAG targets minimal relevant passages [2], while regulatory QA studies show that retrieving all supporting evidence becomes harder when it spans multiple passages [3]. In an end-to-end legal benchmark, retrieval-model choice had a larger effect on answer correctness than generator choice [4].</p>
+<p class="">Evaluation also extends beyond correctness. Legal QA research examines whether answers are supported by the supplied context [5], while ALCE evaluates answer correctness and citation quality separately [6]. Together, these studies motivate evaluating both the evidence available to the generator and the sources ultimately cited.</p>
+<p class="">Our study complements these approaches by holding the generator fixed and examining a broader sequence of pipeline choices, from document processing to final page selection.</p>
+</div></section>
 
-<section aria-labelledby="method-title">
- <div class="section-title"><span class="num">02</span><h2 id="method-title">Methodology</h2></div>
- <p class="data"><strong>Data:</strong> 30 DIFC legal PDFs (590 pages), 100 questions: 70 structured-answer and 30 free-text. Reference source pages are available for 95 questions. In the mixed corpus, 10 PDFs (107 pages) become synthetic scans: page images at 300 DPI with the text layer removed.</p>
