+<section id="s10" class=""><h2><span>10.</span> Hypothesis evaluation</h2>
+<div class="content"><div class="hyp-result h1"><h3>H1 — Retrieval &amp; representation</h3>
+<p class=""><strong>Partially supported.</strong> Multi-scale representations alone reduce Q, but adding lexical retrieval and reranking substantially improves answer quality and source-page recall.</p>
+</div><div class="hyp-result h2"><h3>H2 — Scan robustness</h3>
+<p class=""><strong>Supported.</strong> OCR restores most of the answer-quality loss caused by removing the machine-readable text layer.</p>
+</div><div class="hyp-result h3"><h3>H3 — Citation selection</h3>
+<p class=""><strong>Supported with a trade-off.</strong> Precision rises from 27.1% to 61.8%, while recall decreases moderately from 78.9% to 74.7%.</p>
+</div></div></section>
+
+
+
+
+<section id="s11" class="conclusion"><h2><span>11.</span> Conclusion and takeaways</h2>
+<div class="content"><p class="main-takeaway">Legal RAG should be designed as a multi-stage pipeline: OCR improves robustness to unreadable PDFs, hybrid retrieval and reranking improve answer quality, and post-answer citation selection improves citation precision. However, citation selection introduces a precision–recall trade-off, so answer quality and citation quality should be optimized and evaluated separately.</p>
+<div class="takeaways"><div><h3>1 · Ensure document robustness</h3>
+<p class="">OCR provides robustness when machine-readable text is unavailable. It recovered most of the performance loss introduced by synthetic scans.</p>
+</div><div><h3>2 · Optimize retrieval</h3>
+<p class="">Hybrid retrieval and reranking produced the largest observed gains in answer quality. Additional representation complexity did not consistently improve performance.</p>
+</div><div><h3>3 · Optimize citations separately</h3>
+<p class="">Post-answer citation selection substantially increased precision, with a small reduction in recall.</p>
+</div></div><p class="unexpected"><strong>Unexpected outcome:</strong> Multi-scale representations did not improve performance; Q decreased from 63.2% to 49.8% after R2.</p>
+</div></section><section id="s12" class=""><h2><span>12.</span> Limitations</h2>
+<div class="content"><p class="">one DIFC corpus and 100 questions; one run per condition with no significance testing; LLM-based evaluation of free-text answers.</p>
+</div></section><section id="s13" class=""><h2><span>13.</span> References</h2>
